From c8246829a65411e36f9f531832baf33c8ba3a527 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:41:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(cron): read list_jobs' executions through a kept reader
 keyed on data_version [daveey]

After the jobs.json memo (987876f5d0a), most of the Desktop cron poll's
remaining cost on the serve was list_jobs -> latest_executions +
live_executions: two opens of executions.db per profile per list, each
paying connect + WAL pragma + schema initialize (~2.2 ms of a 5.1 ms
list_jobs on the hub's default store; 0.4-0.6% of a core on the busiest
serve in the t01a1d59e py-spy captures). Card t_01a1d6c7f670c9d8, design
t_01a1d563324d7e8e section 6.

list_jobs now calls cron.executions.listed_executions, which reads through
one kept read-only connection per ledger file and memoizes each job's
latest row and every claimed/running row on that connection's
PRAGMA data_version. data_version moves on every commit by any other
connection, and every ledger write opens its own, so a start, a finish or
a foreign process's write is seen by the next list; an unchanged ledger
costs one pragma and no open. Owner liveness is not in the file and is
re-checked on every call. A replaced file or -shm (new inode), a sqlite
error, a missing file (the exact path then creates it, as before),
Windows, or a fork drops to the exact per-call reads. Other callers of
latest_executions/live_executions keep their exact per-call reads.

P1 pass cron.executions counts a memo hit as a skip, a recompute as a
change, and an exact-path read as a fallback.

RED->GREEN: tests/cron/test_list_jobs_executions_memo.py (3 fail on the
base: no open on an unchanged ledger, listed_executions, P1 skips).
Mutants killed: never invalidating the memo (3 tests), skipping the
inode/-shm check (replaced-file test).
